1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:02,716
It's a hot day in early September

2
00:00:02,716 --> 00:00:04,527
of nineteen sixty-six.

3
00:00:04,707 --> 00:00:07,252
Your father pulls the family car up

4
00:00:07,252 --> 00:00:09,433
in front of a brick dormitory.

5
00:00:09,613 --> 00:00:10,590
In the trunk,

6
00:00:10,590 --> 00:00:12,019
there's a suitcase,

7
00:00:12,019 --> 00:00:14,125
a typewriter, a clock radio,

8
00:00:14,125 --> 00:00:16,156
a reading lamp and a box of

9
00:00:16,156 --> 00:00:18,111
sheets your mother ironed.

10
00:00:18,291 --> 00:00:20,056
You're eighteen years old.

11
00:00:20,236 --> 00:00:22,247
And you're about to become the first

12
00:00:22,247 --> 00:00:23,922
person in your family to go to

13
00:00:23,922 --> 00:00:24,369
college.

14
00:00:24,719 --> 00:00:27,707
Millions of young Americans made that same

15
00:00:27,707 --> 00:00:29,130
trip in the sixties.

16
00:00:29,310 --> 00:00:30,615
In nineteen sixty,

17
00:00:30,615 --> 00:00:33,513
about three and a half million Americans

18
00:00:33,513 --> 00:00:35,324
were enrolled in college.

19
00:00:35,504 --> 00:00:36,934
By nineteen seventy,

20
00:00:36,934 --> 00:00:39,150
it was more than eight million.

21
00:00:39,330 --> 00:00:41,795
Colleges were building new dorms,

22
00:00:41,795 --> 00:00:44,783
new classrooms and whole new campuses as

23
00:00:44,783 --> 00:00:46,203
fast as they could.

24
00:00:46,553 --> 00:00:48,732
And if you started college in the

25
00:00:48,732 --> 00:00:49,921
fall of sixty-six,

26
00:00:49,921 --> 00:00:52,233
you were about to live through four

27
00:00:52,233 --> 00:00:54,743
of the most dramatic years in American

28
00:00:54,743 --> 00:00:55,272
history.

29
00:00:55,452 --> 00:00:58,168
Let's follow a freshman from that first

30
00:00:58,168 --> 00:01:00,885
day on campus to graduation in nineteen

31
00:01:00,885 --> 00:01:01,442
seventy.

32
00:01:01,793 --> 00:01:03,499
Before you even arrived,

33
00:01:03,499 --> 00:01:05,775
there was the acceptance letter.

34
00:01:05,955 --> 00:01:07,274
It came in the mail,

35
00:01:07,274 --> 00:01:09,319
in a thick envelope if the news

36
00:01:09,319 --> 00:01:11,100
was good, and a thin one if

37
00:01:11,100 --> 00:01:11,760
it wasn't.

38
00:01:11,940 --> 00:01:14,894
Most students applied to only one or

39
00:01:14,894 --> 00:01:18,341
two schools, often the state university or

40
00:01:18,341 --> 00:01:20,311
a college close to home.

41
00:01:20,491 --> 00:01:23,406
And many chose their school simply because

42
00:01:23,406 --> 00:01:25,558
a parent, a teacher or an older

43
00:01:25,558 --> 00:01:27,085
cousin had gone there.

44
00:01:27,435 --> 00:01:30,662
College in nineteen sixty-six had a lot

45
00:01:30,662 --> 00:01:33,228
of rules, especially for women.

46
00:01:33,408 --> 00:01:35,707
Most campuses were still run on a

47
00:01:35,707 --> 00:01:38,075
principle called in loco parentis,

48
00:01:38,075 --> 00:01:40,722
which meant the college acted in place

49
00:01:40,722 --> 00:01:41,837
of your parents.

50
00:01:42,017 --> 00:01:44,023
Women's dorms had curfews,

51
00:01:44,023 --> 00:01:47,264
often ten or eleven o'clock on weeknights.

52
00:01:47,444 --> 00:01:49,833
A housemother at the front desk checked

53
00:01:49,833 --> 00:01:50,568
everyone in.

54
00:01:50,918 --> 00:01:53,465
Men were not allowed in women's dorms,

55
00:01:53,465 --> 00:01:54,806
except in the lobby,

56
00:01:54,806 --> 00:01:56,281
during visiting hours.

57
00:01:56,461 --> 00:01:57,693
On some campuses,

58
00:01:57,693 --> 00:01:59,940
if a man visited a woman's room

59
00:01:59,940 --> 00:02:01,969
on a special open house day,

60
00:02:01,969 --> 00:02:03,853
the door had to stay open,

61
00:02:03,853 --> 00:02:06,245
and everyone had to keep at least

62
00:02:06,245 --> 00:02:07,839
one foot on the floor.

63
00:02:08,019 --> 00:02:10,276
Women who came in late could be

64
00:02:10,276 --> 00:02:12,315
punished, or even sent home.

65
00:02:12,664 --> 00:02:15,335
Many schools still had dress codes.

66
00:02:15,515 --> 00:02:17,612
Women wore skirts to class and to

67
00:02:17,612 --> 00:02:18,057
dinner.

68
00:02:18,237 --> 00:02:21,065
Men wore slacks and collared shirts,

69
00:02:21,065 --> 00:02:23,656
and sometimes a jacket and tie in

70
00:02:23,656 --> 00:02:24,913
the dining hall.

71
00:02:25,093 --> 00:02:26,267
At some schools,

72
00:02:26,267 --> 00:02:29,054
freshmen even had to wear little caps,

73
00:02:29,054 --> 00:02:32,062
called beanies, during their first weeks,

74
00:02:32,062 --> 00:02:34,776
so upperclassmen could pick them out.

75
00:02:35,125 --> 00:02:37,779
Meals were served in a big dining

76
00:02:37,779 --> 00:02:39,548
hall, cafeteria style,

77
00:02:39,548 --> 00:02:40,594
at set times.

78
00:02:40,774 --> 00:02:43,088
Miss dinner and you went hungry,

79
00:02:43,088 --> 00:02:45,257
or walked to the snack bar for

80
00:02:45,257 --> 00:02:46,486
a grilled cheese.

81
00:02:46,666 --> 00:02:48,547
The food was famously bad.

82
00:02:48,727 --> 00:02:52,339
Mystery meat, soggy vegetables and Jell-O in

83
00:02:52,339 --> 00:02:53,324
every color.

84
00:02:53,504 --> 00:02:55,680
Students complained endlessly,

85
00:02:55,680 --> 00:02:57,928
and then went back for seconds.

86
00:02:58,278 --> 00:02:59,845
What did college cost?

87
00:03:00,025 --> 00:03:01,607
In nineteen sixty-six,

88
00:03:01,607 --> 00:03:04,340
the average yearly tuition at a public

89
00:03:04,340 --> 00:03:07,217
four-year college was only a few hundred

90
00:03:07,217 --> 00:03:07,792
dollars.

91
00:03:07,972 --> 00:03:10,387
Room and board added maybe seven or

92
00:03:10,387 --> 00:03:11,699
eight hundred more.

93
00:03:11,879 --> 00:03:14,231
A whole year at a state university

94
00:03:14,231 --> 00:03:17,068
could cost around twelve hundred dollars.

95
00:03:17,248 --> 00:03:18,606
At a private college,

96
00:03:18,606 --> 00:03:20,093
it might be twice that.

97
00:03:20,442 --> 00:03:22,385
That sounds incredible today,

98
00:03:22,385 --> 00:03:25,198
but the minimum wage in nineteen sixty-six

99
00:03:25,198 --> 00:03:27,408
was a dollar twenty-five an hour.

100
00:03:27,588 --> 00:03:30,804
Even so, many students paid their own

101
00:03:30,804 --> 00:03:32,716
way with a summer job,

102
00:03:32,716 --> 00:03:34,542
working in a factory,

103
00:03:34,542 --> 00:03:36,628
at a resort or on a road

104
00:03:36,628 --> 00:03:37,062
crew.

105
00:03:37,242 --> 00:03:39,576
They graduated with little or no debt.

106
00:03:39,756 --> 00:03:42,579
Today, the average yearly cost at a

107
00:03:42,579 --> 00:03:45,725
public university is many times higher,

108
00:03:45,725 --> 00:03:47,418
even after inflation.

109
00:03:47,768 --> 00:03:48,607
And for many,

110
00:03:48,607 --> 00:03:51,124
college was paid for by the government.

111
00:03:51,304 --> 00:03:54,648
The Higher Education Act of nineteen sixty-five

112
00:03:54,648 --> 00:03:57,495
created new grants and student loans for

113
00:03:57,495 --> 00:04:00,127
families who couldn't afford college.

114
00:04:00,307 --> 00:04:01,705
For the first time,

115
00:04:01,705 --> 00:04:03,986
millions of working-class kids,

116
00:04:03,986 --> 00:04:07,002
the sons and daughters of factory workers

117
00:04:07,002 --> 00:04:08,620
and farmers, could go.

118
00:04:08,970 --> 00:04:11,397
Dorm rooms were small and simple.

119
00:04:11,577 --> 00:04:12,673
Two metal beds,

120
00:04:12,673 --> 00:04:15,159
two desks, two dressers and a sink

121
00:04:15,159 --> 00:04:16,182
in the corner,

122
00:04:16,182 --> 00:04:17,498
if you were lucky.

123
00:04:17,678 --> 00:04:19,502
Your roommate was a stranger,

124
00:04:19,502 --> 00:04:20,885
picked by the college,

125
00:04:20,885 --> 00:04:22,772
and you met for the first time

126
00:04:22,772 --> 00:04:23,715
on move-in day.

127
00:04:23,895 --> 00:04:26,682
Some roommates became lifelong friends.

128
00:04:26,862 --> 00:04:28,766
Others barely lasted a week.

129
00:04:29,115 --> 00:04:31,661
There was one telephone on each floor,

130
00:04:31,661 --> 00:04:33,469
a pay phone in the hallway.

131
00:04:33,649 --> 00:04:34,573
When it rang,

132
00:04:34,573 --> 00:04:37,770
whoever was closest answered and shouted down

133
00:04:37,770 --> 00:04:38,409
the hall.

134
00:04:38,589 --> 00:04:41,559
Long distance calls home were expensive,

135
00:04:41,559 --> 00:04:43,861
so students called once a week,

136
00:04:43,861 --> 00:04:45,643
usually on Sunday night,

137
00:04:45,643 --> 00:04:47,424
when rates were cheaper.

138
00:04:47,604 --> 00:04:49,253
Parents waited by the phone.

139
00:04:49,603 --> 00:04:51,833
Most of the communication was by letter.

140
00:04:52,013 --> 00:04:54,279
Students wrote home every week,

141
00:04:54,279 --> 00:04:55,960
often asking for money.

142
00:04:56,140 --> 00:04:59,159
Parents sent back letters with a check,

143
00:04:59,159 --> 00:05:00,861
a few dollars in cash,

144
00:05:00,861 --> 00:05:03,803
or a care package of homemade cookies.

145
00:05:03,983 --> 00:05:06,235
Getting mail was the highlight of the

146
00:05:06,235 --> 00:05:06,479
day.

147
00:05:06,659 --> 00:05:09,196
An empty mailbox could ruin a whole

148
00:05:09,196 --> 00:05:09,921
afternoon.

149
00:05:10,271 --> 00:05:11,409
And there was laundry.

150
00:05:11,589 --> 00:05:12,881
For many young men,

151
00:05:12,881 --> 00:05:15,261
college was the first time they had

152
00:05:15,261 --> 00:05:17,301
ever washed their own clothes.

153
00:05:17,481 --> 00:05:19,959
Some mailed their laundry home in a

154
00:05:19,959 --> 00:05:21,376
special canvas case,

155
00:05:21,376 --> 00:05:24,066
and their mothers mailed it back clean

156
00:05:24,066 --> 00:05:27,536
and folded, sometimes with cookies tucked inside.

157
00:05:27,716 --> 00:05:29,837
Others just let it pile up until

158
00:05:29,837 --> 00:05:31,361
it couldn't be ignored.

159
00:05:31,711 --> 00:05:33,093
Classes were big.

160
00:05:33,273 --> 00:05:36,695
Freshman lectures might have several hundred students

161
00:05:36,695 --> 00:05:37,664
in a huge hall,

162
00:05:37,664 --> 00:05:39,794
with a professor at the front and

163
00:05:39,794 --> 00:05:40,634
a microphone.

164
00:05:40,814 --> 00:05:43,232
Students took notes by hand,

165
00:05:43,232 --> 00:05:44,959
in spiral notebooks.

166
00:05:45,139 --> 00:05:47,542
Papers were typed on a manual or

167
00:05:47,542 --> 00:05:50,847
electric typewriter, with carbon paper for a

168
00:05:50,847 --> 00:05:51,222
copy.

169
00:05:51,402 --> 00:05:54,463
A typing mistake meant correction fluid,

170
00:05:54,463 --> 00:05:56,452
or starting the page over.

171
00:05:56,802 --> 00:05:59,428
The library was the center of the

172
00:05:59,428 --> 00:06:00,622
academic world.

173
00:06:00,802 --> 00:06:02,869
No internet, no computers.

174
00:06:03,049 --> 00:06:05,733
Students searched the card catalog,

175
00:06:05,733 --> 00:06:08,493
a huge wooden cabinet full of little

176
00:06:08,493 --> 00:06:11,331
drawers, and then hunted for books in

177
00:06:11,331 --> 00:06:12,174
the stacks.

178
00:06:12,354 --> 00:06:14,971
Research for a term paper could take

179
00:06:14,971 --> 00:06:15,408
weeks.

180
00:06:15,588 --> 00:06:19,230
During finals, the library stayed open late,

181
00:06:19,230 --> 00:06:21,300
and every chair was full.

182
00:06:21,649 --> 00:06:24,839
Calculations were done with a slide rule,

183
00:06:24,839 --> 00:06:27,640
a ruler with a sliding middle piece.

184
00:06:27,820 --> 00:06:30,636
Engineering students carried them in a leather

185
00:06:30,636 --> 00:06:31,860
case on their belts.

186
00:06:32,040 --> 00:06:35,823
Electronic calculators wouldn't be affordable until the

187
00:06:35,823 --> 00:06:36,510
seventies.

188
00:06:36,690 --> 00:06:40,671
Computers existed, but they filled entire rooms,

189
00:06:40,671 --> 00:06:43,988
and students fed them programs on stacks

190
00:06:43,988 --> 00:06:45,398
of punched cards.

191
00:06:45,747 --> 00:06:48,058
Grades mattered more than ever.

192
00:06:48,238 --> 00:06:51,308
A bad semester could mean losing a

193
00:06:51,308 --> 00:06:54,107
scholarship, or, for young men,

194
00:06:54,107 --> 00:06:56,365
losing a draft deferment.

195
00:06:56,545 --> 00:07:00,317
Students pulled all-nighters fueled by black coffee

196
00:07:00,317 --> 00:07:01,723
and No-Doz tablets.

197
00:07:01,903 --> 00:07:03,404
During exam week,

198
00:07:03,404 --> 00:07:05,258
the dorms went quiet.

199
00:07:05,438 --> 00:07:07,443
And when grades were posted on a

200
00:07:07,443 --> 00:07:09,323
bulletin board in the hallway,

201
00:07:09,323 --> 00:07:11,891
crowds gathered to look for their student

202
00:07:11,891 --> 00:07:12,393
numbers.

203
00:07:12,742 --> 00:07:15,540
Social life revolved around the weekend.

204
00:07:15,720 --> 00:07:18,723
Fraternities and sororities were at the height

205
00:07:18,723 --> 00:07:20,028
of their popularity.

206
00:07:20,208 --> 00:07:22,530
Rush week came every fall.

207
00:07:22,710 --> 00:07:23,902
There were mixers,

208
00:07:23,902 --> 00:07:26,155
where boys from one dorm met girls

209
00:07:26,155 --> 00:07:28,143
from another, with a band or a

210
00:07:28,143 --> 00:07:30,396
record player and punch in a bowl.

211
00:07:30,576 --> 00:07:32,714
Homecoming was a huge event,

212
00:07:32,714 --> 00:07:33,784
with a parade,

213
00:07:33,784 --> 00:07:35,846
floats and a football game.

214
00:07:36,196 --> 00:07:37,799
On Saturday afternoons,

215
00:07:37,799 --> 00:07:40,376
the whole campus went to the football

216
00:07:40,376 --> 00:07:40,724
game.

217
00:07:40,904 --> 00:07:43,227
Students wore their school colors,

218
00:07:43,227 --> 00:07:44,729
cheered with the band,

219
00:07:44,729 --> 00:07:46,164
and carried pennants.

220
00:07:46,344 --> 00:07:48,561
In winter, it was basketball.

221
00:07:48,741 --> 00:07:50,963
Afterward, there were parties,

222
00:07:50,963 --> 00:07:53,927
dances and late-night trips to the diner

223
00:07:53,927 --> 00:07:55,556
for burgers and fries.

224
00:07:55,906 --> 00:07:57,218
And there was the music.

225
00:07:57,398 --> 00:08:00,767
In nineteen sixty-six and sixty-seven,

226
00:08:00,767 --> 00:08:05,199
college students listened to the Beatles' Revolver

227
00:08:05,199 --> 00:08:05,909
and Sgt.

228
00:08:06,089 --> 00:08:07,811
Pepper's, Bob Dylan,

229
00:08:07,811 --> 00:08:09,534
Simon and Garfunkel,

230
00:08:09,534 --> 00:08:11,860
the Beach Boys' Pet Sounds,

231
00:08:11,860 --> 00:08:13,066
and the Doors.

232
00:08:13,246 --> 00:08:15,417
Every dorm room had a record player.

233
00:08:15,597 --> 00:08:16,863
And late at night,

234
00:08:16,863 --> 00:08:19,815
students listened to new FM radio stations

235
00:08:19,815 --> 00:08:21,925
that played whole album sides.

236
00:08:22,274 --> 00:08:24,306
Dating had its own rituals.

237
00:08:24,486 --> 00:08:26,872
A man asked a woman out days

238
00:08:26,872 --> 00:08:29,769
in advance, by phone or in person.

239
00:08:29,949 --> 00:08:31,673
He picked her up at the dorm

240
00:08:31,673 --> 00:08:34,321
lobby, signed her out with the housemother,

241
00:08:34,321 --> 00:08:36,230
and had her back before curfew.

242
00:08:36,410 --> 00:08:37,809
If they got serious,

243
00:08:37,809 --> 00:08:39,977
he gave her his fraternity pin,

244
00:08:39,977 --> 00:08:42,215
which was called getting pinned.

245
00:08:42,395 --> 00:08:44,754
It was the step before engagement,

246
00:08:44,754 --> 00:08:48,084
and many couples married right after graduation.

247
00:08:48,433 --> 00:08:50,362
For young men in college in the

248
00:08:50,362 --> 00:08:52,849
late sixties, there was one thing always

249
00:08:52,849 --> 00:08:54,529
in the back of their minds.

250
00:08:54,709 --> 00:08:55,521
The draft.

251
00:08:55,701 --> 00:08:58,116
The war in Vietnam was growing.

252
00:08:58,296 --> 00:08:59,794
In nineteen sixty-six,

253
00:08:59,794 --> 00:09:02,587
the United States had about three hundred

254
00:09:02,587 --> 00:09:04,561
eighty thousand troops there,

255
00:09:04,561 --> 00:09:06,400
and the number kept rising.

256
00:09:06,580 --> 00:09:09,763
Every month, thousands of young men were

257
00:09:09,763 --> 00:09:10,400
drafted.

258
00:09:10,749 --> 00:09:13,859
College students could get a student deferment,

259
00:09:13,859 --> 00:09:14,852
called a two-S,

260
00:09:14,852 --> 00:09:17,565
which postponed the draft while they were

261
00:09:17,565 --> 00:09:18,226
in school.

262
00:09:18,406 --> 00:09:20,251
But they had to stay enrolled and

263
00:09:20,251 --> 00:09:21,425
keep their grades up.

264
00:09:21,605 --> 00:09:23,869
Some men stayed in school,

265
00:09:23,869 --> 00:09:26,481
or went on to graduate school,

266
00:09:26,481 --> 00:09:29,790
partly to avoid being sent to Vietnam.

267
00:09:29,970 --> 00:09:32,046
Flunking out could mean a ticket to

268
00:09:32,046 --> 00:09:32,698
the jungle.

269
00:09:33,048 --> 00:09:35,416
In December of nineteen sixty-nine,

270
00:09:35,416 --> 00:09:38,325
the government held the first draft lottery

271
00:09:38,325 --> 00:09:39,678
since World War Two.

272
00:09:39,858 --> 00:09:41,758
Every birthday was drawn,

273
00:09:41,758 --> 00:09:42,594
one by one,

274
00:09:42,594 --> 00:09:44,037
on live television.

275
00:09:44,217 --> 00:09:46,965
Young men gathered in dorm lounges to

276
00:09:46,965 --> 00:09:47,410
watch.

277
00:09:47,590 --> 00:09:49,595
If your birthday came up with a

278
00:09:49,595 --> 00:09:51,728
low number, you were likely to be

279
00:09:51,728 --> 00:09:52,246
drafted.

280
00:09:52,426 --> 00:09:53,516
If it was high,

281
00:09:53,516 --> 00:09:55,189
you were probably safe.

282
00:09:55,369 --> 00:09:56,988
It was one of the most tense

283
00:09:56,988 --> 00:09:58,260
nights of their lives.

284
00:09:58,609 --> 00:10:01,867
By nineteen sixty-seven and sixty-eight,

285
00:10:01,867 --> 00:10:04,147
campuses were changing fast.

286
00:10:04,327 --> 00:10:06,153
Students protested the war,

287
00:10:06,153 --> 00:10:07,844
marched for civil rights,

288
00:10:07,844 --> 00:10:10,076
and demanded a voice in how their

289
00:10:10,076 --> 00:10:11,293
colleges were run.

290
00:10:11,473 --> 00:10:15,223
Teach-ins, rallies and sit-ins became common.

291
00:10:15,403 --> 00:10:17,795
Some students joined the protests.

292
00:10:17,975 --> 00:10:19,729
Many more simply watched,

293
00:10:19,729 --> 00:10:21,133
studied and worried.

294
00:10:21,482 --> 00:10:23,421
Styles changed too.

295
00:10:23,601 --> 00:10:26,854
Freshmen who arrived in sixty-six with crew

296
00:10:26,854 --> 00:10:30,258
cuts and pleated skirts looked very different

297
00:10:30,258 --> 00:10:31,090
by seventy.

298
00:10:31,270 --> 00:10:32,477
Hair got longer.

299
00:10:32,657 --> 00:10:34,085
Skirts got shorter,

300
00:10:34,085 --> 00:10:35,362
then much longer.

301
00:10:35,542 --> 00:10:39,083
Jeans and sandals replaced slacks and loafers.

302
00:10:39,263 --> 00:10:40,470
And one by one,

303
00:10:40,470 --> 00:10:43,446
the old rules about curfews and dress

304
00:10:43,446 --> 00:10:44,894
codes disappeared.

305
00:10:45,244 --> 00:10:47,915
Nineteen sixty-eight was the hardest year.

306
00:10:48,095 --> 00:10:51,235
In April, Martin Luther King Junior was

307
00:10:51,235 --> 00:10:51,798
killed.

308
00:10:51,978 --> 00:10:54,846
In June, Robert Kennedy was killed.

309
00:10:55,026 --> 00:10:57,724
Students who had grown up believing in

310
00:10:57,724 --> 00:11:00,422
a bright future suddenly saw a country

311
00:11:00,422 --> 00:11:01,203
in turmoil.

312
00:11:01,383 --> 00:11:03,403
Many say that year changed them more

313
00:11:03,403 --> 00:11:05,086
than any class they ever took.

314
00:11:05,437 --> 00:11:07,593
In July of nineteen sixty-nine,

315
00:11:07,593 --> 00:11:10,236
many students were home for the summer

316
00:11:10,236 --> 00:11:12,879
when Apollo eleven landed on the moon.

317
00:11:13,059 --> 00:11:15,391
They watched it on the family television,

318
00:11:15,391 --> 00:11:17,781
with their parents and little brothers and

319
00:11:17,781 --> 00:11:18,237
sisters.

320
00:11:18,417 --> 00:11:20,673
After everything that had happened,

321
00:11:20,673 --> 00:11:22,608
it was a moment of pure pride.

322
00:11:22,788 --> 00:11:23,632
For one night,

323
00:11:23,632 --> 00:11:25,983
the whole country seemed to be cheering

324
00:11:25,983 --> 00:11:26,526
together.

325
00:11:26,877 --> 00:11:29,557
And then, in the spring of nineteen

326
00:11:29,557 --> 00:11:32,926
seventy, the class that started in sixty-six

327
00:11:32,926 --> 00:11:33,692
graduated.

328
00:11:33,872 --> 00:11:35,808
They had arrived in a world of

329
00:11:35,808 --> 00:11:37,937
beanies and curfews and left in a

330
00:11:37,937 --> 00:11:40,454
world that had been turned upside down.

331
00:11:40,634 --> 00:11:43,206
That May, the shootings at Kent State

332
00:11:43,206 --> 00:11:46,126
in Ohio shook campuses across the country.

333
00:11:46,306 --> 00:11:50,174
Some schools canceled classes and even graduation

334
00:11:50,174 --> 00:11:51,043
ceremonies.

335
00:11:51,393 --> 00:11:53,728
But most of them walked across the

336
00:11:53,728 --> 00:11:56,064
stage, shook the president's hand,

337
00:11:56,064 --> 00:11:57,644
and took their diploma,

338
00:11:57,644 --> 00:12:00,530
while their parents cried in the audience.

339
00:12:00,710 --> 00:12:02,775
For many, they were the first in

340
00:12:02,775 --> 00:12:05,551
their family ever to graduate from college.

341
00:12:05,731 --> 00:12:07,675
It was the proudest day of their

342
00:12:07,675 --> 00:12:08,587
parents' lives.

343
00:12:08,937 --> 00:12:11,809
The class of nineteen seventy went on

344
00:12:11,809 --> 00:12:13,284
to become teachers,

345
00:12:13,284 --> 00:12:15,381
engineers, lawyers, nurses,

346
00:12:15,381 --> 00:12:17,632
business owners and soldiers.

347
00:12:17,812 --> 00:12:19,235
They raised families,

348
00:12:19,235 --> 00:12:22,418
built careers and lived through everything that

349
00:12:22,418 --> 00:12:23,095
came next.

350
00:12:23,275 --> 00:12:25,254
But many say those four years,

351
00:12:25,254 --> 00:12:26,970
from sixty-six to seventy,

352
00:12:26,970 --> 00:12:29,147
shaped who they were for the rest

353
00:12:29,147 --> 00:12:30,136
of their lives.

354
00:12:30,487 --> 00:12:33,238
Today, college looks very different.

355
00:12:33,418 --> 00:12:34,544
No curfews.

356
00:12:34,724 --> 00:12:35,839
No beanies.

357
00:12:36,019 --> 00:12:38,236
Computers in every pocket.

358
00:12:38,416 --> 00:12:39,926
But it costs far more,

359
00:12:39,926 --> 00:12:42,603
and many students graduate with tens of

360
00:12:42,603 --> 00:12:44,593
thousands of dollars in debt.

361
00:12:44,773 --> 00:12:46,457
In nineteen sixty-six,

362
00:12:46,457 --> 00:12:49,212
a young person could pay for college

363
00:12:49,212 --> 00:12:50,589
with a summer job.

364
00:12:50,769 --> 00:12:52,615
That's almost impossible today.

365
00:12:52,966 --> 00:12:55,247
If you started college in the late

366
00:12:55,247 --> 00:12:56,925
sixties, you remember it.

367
00:12:57,105 --> 00:12:59,159
The typewriter in your dorm room.

368
00:12:59,339 --> 00:13:01,000
The pay phone in the hall.

369
00:13:01,180 --> 00:13:03,877
The letters from home with a five-dollar

370
00:13:03,877 --> 00:13:04,686
bill inside.

371
00:13:04,866 --> 00:13:06,549
The roommate you'll never forget.

372
00:13:06,729 --> 00:13:08,738
And the feeling that the whole world

373
00:13:08,738 --> 00:13:10,692
was changing, and you were right in

374
00:13:10,692 --> 00:13:11,640
the middle of it.

375
00:13:11,991 --> 00:13:13,813
So tell us in the comments.

376
00:13:13,993 --> 00:13:15,520
Where did you go to college,

377
00:13:15,520 --> 00:13:17,047
and what year did you start?

378
00:13:17,227 --> 00:13:18,697
What was your dorm like,

379
00:13:18,697 --> 00:13:20,536
and what do you remember most?

380
00:13:20,716 --> 00:13:22,190
We read every comment.

381
00:13:22,370 --> 00:13:24,523
Next time, we're looking at the truth

382
00:13:24,523 --> 00:13:27,199
about the nineteen sixties kitchen your mother

383
00:13:27,199 --> 00:13:27,664
trusted.

384
00:13:27,844 --> 00:13:29,584
Thank you for spending this time with

385
00:13:29,584 --> 00:13:29,725
me.
